Make the echo controller anew after a change of audio devices

Patch 0010 keeps what AEC3 learned of the room when a microphone is turned on again in a
call: with a headset, that there is no echo. A headset taken off for speakers while the
microphone was off then kept that, and the other side heard themselves for seconds after
the unmute, until AEC3 found the echo (figures below).

Whatever learns of a change of audio devices now marks it, from any thread
(MarkAudioDevicesChanged, api/audio/audio_device.h: a count for the library), and
AudioState makes the echo controller anew at the next start of recording when the count
moved since recording last started, rather than restarting it. A change while recording
runs counts at the next start too. A start that fails takes nothing. False marks cost what
0009 cost: one unmute on a headset holds the microphone down for its first seconds.

Marked here:
- PulseAudio (Linux): a sink or source added or removed, a change of the server's default
  output or input (asked for and compared: the server tells of other changes too), a
  change of a sink's or source's active port (asked for and compared, per device: an
  analog jack switching the same card from headphones to speakers; a volume change is a
  change too and does not count), one of the module's own streams moved by the server to
  another device, and the server lost or back (TellDevicesUpdated). Subscribed from the
  connection on (SubscribeToDevices), and again when the server comes back.
- Windows Core Audio: an endpoint added, removed or changing state, and a change of the
  default device (IMMNotificationClient), from the thread that tells them.
The wrapper marks a device chosen (another index than the last asked for) and a change of
the device list (wrapper patch 0021); the macOS engine device marks its own (apple patch
0008).

Known and not tried: a Bluetooth headset on Linux switching profile (A2DP and HFP) as
recording stops and starts removes and adds its sink and source and moves the streams, so
its unmutes most likely still make a new echo controller, as 0009 did (the unmute's fix
does not reach it); a change PulseAudio tells of is marked one round trip to the server
later (the defaults and ports are asked for and compared), a few milliseconds.

Measured in one session of the call lab (mic.sh unmute, UNMUTE_UNPLUG: a's output taken
away while it is muted, its sound falling to the default output, which goes back into its
microphone; the other side talking in speech-like noise; 6 runs each): the other side heard
themselves 0.3 s after the unmute at -25 dBFS at the loudest, as with 0009 (0.3 s, -24 dBFS;
p 1.0 and 0.59), where patch 0010 alone let them hear themselves 0.9 s at -14 dBFS. The
same change with nothing marked (the room's echo added with no device event) stays at
1.1 s and -15 dBFS against 0.2 s and -32 dBFS with 0009: what any version does when a room
changes with the microphone on.

